---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const page = "https://ir.tesla.com/voluntary-retail-voting-program";
const defa = "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1318605/000110465926116716/tm267596d6_defa14a.htm";
---

<Base
  title="Retail voting program"
  description="A neutral explanation of Tesla’s voluntary retail voting program: standing instructions, the two options, and what they do not do."
>
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Voluntary · with the board · not this site’s recommendation</p>
    <h1>The retail voting program, in plain English.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The company adopted a program that lets a retail holder leave a standing instruction to vote
      with the board at later meetings. It is free to join and free to leave. This page does not
      suggest either option.
    </p>
    <p class="source">
      Company page: <a href={page}>ir.tesla.com/voluntary-retail-voting-program</a><br />
      SEC copy of the enrollment screens and terms:
      <a href={defa}>DEFA14A filed October 19, 2026</a>
    </p>

    <h2>What enrolling does</h2>
    <p>
      After a holder votes at the 2026 annual meeting on most electronic platforms, including
      ProxyVote.com and Say by Robinhood, the platform can offer enrollment. The company’s materials
      say you cannot enroll on the investor-relations page itself, because the instruction has to be
      tied to the brokerage account through the proxy distributor.
    </p>
    <p>
      The standing instruction applies to future meetings: any meeting, including an adjournment,
      for which a definitive proxy has <strong>not yet been filed</strong>. The company says it does
      <strong>not</strong> vote the December 3, 2026 annual meeting. A holder still has to vote that
      meeting from its own proxy.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the holder does nothing at a later meeting, the shares covered by the instruction are voted
      with the board recommendation for the matters the holder selected. The holder still receives
      the proxy and can override the instruction by voting. The company’s terms say the most recent
      vote submitted before the polls close takes precedence.
    </p>

    <h2>The two options</h2>
    <div class="grid-2">
      <article class="card">
        <p class="kicker">Option 1</p>
        <h3>All matters</h3>
        <p>
          Eligible shares are voted with the board’s recommendation on each proposal. The enrollment
          screen says that includes every proposal. A merger, acquisition, or sale that is put to
          shareholders would be included.
        </p>
        <p>
          The company also says a holder who picks this option gets a reminder before a meeting that
          includes a “special matter.”
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <p class="kicker">Option 2</p>
        <h3>All matters except certain specified matters</h3>
        <p>
          Same board-aligned instruction, except two kinds of items the company calls special
          matters: a contested director election, and any acquisition, merger, or divestiture that
          state law or exchange rules require shareholders to approve.
        </p>
        <p>
          On those items, this option does not cast the standing instruction. The holder would vote
          them, or leave them unvoted, in the ordinary way.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="source">
      Wording from the enrollment screen in the <a href={defa}>October 19, 2026 DEFA14A</a> and the
      company’s <a href={page}>program page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Leaving, and the timing trap</h2>
    <ul class="clean">
      <li>The terms say a holder can cancel at any time, at no cost, from the account dashboard or from the annual reminder.</li>
      <li>If the cancellation comes after a definitive proxy for a meeting has already been filed, the terms say the cancellation applies to meetings after that one.</li>
      <li>Overriding a single meeting is different from cancelling. Voting the proxy for that meeting overrides the instruction for the proposals voted.</li>
      <li>A reminder goes out at least annually. Option 1 also gets a reminder before a special matter.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="callout">
      <p>
        <strong>A combination proxy filed later would be a “future meeting” under these terms.</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        Option 1 would vote it with the board unless the holder votes that proxy or had already
        opted out before the proxy was filed. Option 2 carves mergers out. None of that is a
        recommendation. Read the terms on the company’s page before choosing.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p><a href="/how-to-vote/">How ballots are delivered, broker by broker.</a></p>
  </div>
</Base>
